## Creating a GitHub Account

- GitHub accounts allow me to store repositories online.

- After creating an account, I can create repositories.

- A repository can be:
    - Public
    - Private

- Public:
    - Anyone can view the repository.

- Private:
    - Only people I give access to can view it.

- Public repositories are useful for:
    - Portfolios
    - Open-source projects
    - Showing employers my code
    - Sharing projects

---

## Creating a GitHub Repository

- On GitHub I can create a new repository.

- I choose:
    - Repository name
    - Description
    - Public/private
    - README if I want one
    - `.gitignore` if appropriate
    - Licence if appropriate

- Example repository:

    python-learning-projects

- GitHub then gives me a remote repository that I can connect to my local Git repository.

---

## Local Repository vs Remote Repository

- My local repository is stored on my computer.

- My remote repository is stored somewhere online, such as GitHub.

- Example:

    My PC
      ↓
    Local Git repository

    GitHub
      ↓
    Remote Git repository

- Git lets me move changes between them.

- Upload changes:

    git push

- Download changes:

    git pull

---

## Git Remote

- A remote is a connection between my local repository and a remote repository.

- I can see my remotes using:

    git remote -v

- I can add a GitHub repository as a remote using:

    git remote add origin <repository-url>

- `origin` is normally the name given to the main remote repository.

- Example:

    git remote add origin https://github.com/myusername/my-project.git

- I can then push my local repository to GitHub.

---

## Git Push

- `git push` uploads my local commits to the remote repository.

- Example:

    git push origin main

- This means:
    - Push my commits
    - To the remote called `origin`
    - To the `main` branch

- Once pushed, my changes should appear on GitHub.

- Typical workflow:

    git add .
    git commit -m "Add new feature"
    git push

---

## Git Pull

- `git pull` downloads changes from the remote repository and applies them to my local repository.

- Example:

    git pull

- This is important when working with other people.

- For example:

    My friend changes the project
        ↓
    Pushes changes to GitHub
        ↓
    I run:
        git pull
        ↓
    I get their changes

---

## Git Fetch

- `git fetch` downloads information about changes from the remote repository without automatically applying those changes to my current branch.

- Example:

    git fetch

- This is useful when I want to see what has changed before merging it into my work.

- Difference:

    git fetch
        = download information

    git pull
        = download + integrate changes

